      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-184015",
      ":question/problem": "Suppose we wish to construct a direct proof of the following statement:\n\n> $2m - 3n$ is even if $m$ is odd and $n$ is even.\n\nA proof schema is outlined below. Fill in the missing entries.\n\nLet $m$ be an {{field-1}} integer and $n$ be an {{field-2}} integer.\nThen,\n$2a + 1$ {{field-3}} {{field-4}}\nfor some {{field-5}}.\n\nBy substituting the above into {{field-6}}, we can show that\n$2c$ {{field-7}}\nwhere $c$ is {{field-8}}.\n\nTherefore, since $2m - 3n$ {{field-9}} a multiple of $2$, we conclude that $2m - 3n$ is {{field-10}}."
    },
